{"name":"Canyon Wall","hex":"#a14935","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"canyon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A valley, especially a long, narrow, steep valley, cut in rock by a river."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Of water, liquid, or another substance, to flow in or into a canyon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/canyon"},{"term":"wall","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rampart of earth, stones etc. built up for defensive purposes.","A structure built for defense surrounding a city, castle etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To enclose with, or as if with, a wall or walls.","To use a wallhack."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wall"}],"entries":[{"title":"Canyon","blocks":[{"text":"A canyon, gorge or chasm, is a deep cleft between escarpments or cliffs resulting from weathering and the erosive activity of a river over geologic time scales. Rivers have a natural tendency to cut through underlying surfaces, eventually wearing away rock layers as sediments are removed downstream. A river bed will gradually reach a baseline elevation, which is the same elevation as the body of water into which the river drains. The processes of weathering and erosion will form canyons when the river's headwaters and estuary are at significantly different elevations, particularly through regions where softer rock layers are intermingled with harder layers more resistant to weathering."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Aerial_view_of_canyons.jpg/330px-Aerial_view_of_canyons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canyon"},{"title":"Wall","blocks":[{"text":"A wall is a structure and a surface that defines an area; carries a load; provides security, shelter, or soundproofing; or serves a decorative purpose. There are various types of walls, including border barriers between countries, brick walls, defensive walls in fortifications, and retaining walls that hold back dirt, stone, water, or noise. Walls can also be found in buildings, where they support roofs, floors, and ceilings, enclose spaces, and provide shelter and security."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Noel_2005_P%C3%A9kin_031_muraille_de_chine_Mutianyu.jpg/330px-Noel_2005_P%C3%A9kin_031_muraille_de_chine_Mutianyu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wall"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Canyon_sumidero_entrada.jpg/500px-Canyon_sumidero_entrada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Canyon_sumidero_entrada.jpg/1280px-Canyon_sumidero_entrada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Canyon_sumidero_entrada.jpg/500px-Canyon_sumidero_entrada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sumidero Canyon, Mexico","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanyon_sumidero_entrada.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Kevo_Canyon.JPG/500px-Kevo_Canyon.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Kevo_Canyon.JPG/1280px-Kevo_Canyon.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Kevo_Canyon.JPG/500px-Kevo_Canyon.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kevo Canyon in Utsjoki, Finland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKevo_Canyon.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Snake_River_Canyon_Idaho_2007.jpg/500px-Snake_River